// Practice quiz: Testing a plain function with Vitest
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/testing-a-plain-function-with-vitest

::o1 structure::In a Vitest test, what does expect(total(2)).toBe(4) state? {
=The call total(2) should produce a value identical to 4 #Correct. expect receives the actual value, and the matcher gives the expected one.
~total should be called 4 times #Not quite. That would need a mock and a different matcher.
~4 should be passed to total #Not quite. The argument is 2.
~The test should run for 4 seconds #Not quite. It has nothing to do with time.
}

::o1 what to test::Which function is easiest to test with a plain unit test? {
=One that calculates a total from its arguments #Correct. A pure function needs no page or network.
~One that reads from a live database #Not quite. That needs a running service.
~One that changes the DOM of a page #Not quite. That needs a DOM environment.
~One that depends on the current time #Not quite. Its result changes between runs.
}

::o2 reading failure::A failure shows Expected 12 and Received 11. Which value did your code produce? {
=11 #Correct. Received is what your code returned.
~12 #Not quite. Expected is what the test asked for.
~23 #Not quite. Nothing adds them.
~It cannot be known #Not quite. The output states it directly.
}

::o2 run script::package.json has "test"\: "vitest". Which command starts it? {
=npm test #Correct. npm runs the test script.
~npm install vitest #Not quite. That installs it and does not run it.
~node vitest #Not quite. That tries to run a file named vitest.
~vitest.test #Not quite. That is not a command.
}

::o3 matcher choice::A function returns a new object. Which assertion compares its contents? {
=toEqual #Correct. It compares recursively by contents.
~toBe #Not quite. It needs the same object.
~toThrow #Not quite. That checks for an error.
~toBeTruthy #Not quite. Vitest has this, but it only checks truthiness, not contents.
}
